messages:
Izzy Park: Heads-up: user report — quickstart README TypeScript snippet used `import Redwood from 'redwood-inference'` and errored in ESM projects (no esModuleInterop). Error: `TypeError: Redwood is not a constructor`.

Marco Ruiz: That's the old default import. Our package exports a named factory now. Can you paste the snippet?

Izzy Park: Current README shows:
```ts
import Redwood from 'redwood-inference';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
```

Maya Chen: Fix: use named import and show both CJS + ESM examples:
```ts
// ESM / TS (recommended)
import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference';
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });

// CommonJS
const { createClient } = require('redwood-inference');
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
```

Izzy Park: I'll open a docs PR to update the README, add a one-line FAQ about `esModuleInterop`, and include a tiny smoke CI step (npm pack + minimal TS ESM run).

doc-bot: Issue created: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/issues/999 "quickstart: use named createClient import + CJS/ESM examples"

Marco Ruiz: Please add a short note to the internal onboarding checklist for demos so SEs don't hit this live.

Izzy Park: Added checklist update in the PR description. PR opened and CI started.

Maya Chen: Thanks — ping me for review.
